# NriPrelude And Elm Style

- This project uses `nri-prelude` from the pinned `source-repository-package` in `cabal.project`.
- Prefer `NoImplicitPrelude` plus `import NriPrelude` in Haskell modules.
- Reach for `Text`, `List`, `Maybe`, and `Result` APIs from `nri-prelude` before `Prelude`, `Data.Text`, `Data.List`, `Data.Maybe`, or `Either`.
- Favor small pure functions, explicit top-level type signatures, and pipeline-oriented code with `|>`.
- Model domain failures with custom types and `Result` values instead of exceptions when practical.
- Keep formatting Elm-like: 4-space indentation, leading commas in multiline lists/imports/exports, and an 80-column target via `.fourmolu.yaml`.
- Preserve this style in code, examples, docs, and build config unless the user explicitly asks to change it.

## Module-header import shape

Every module starts with the same three-line prelude block:

```haskell
import qualified Data.Text as T   -- only if Text appears qualified
import NriPrelude
import qualified Prelude
```

`import qualified Prelude` is load-bearing - many base names that
`NriPrelude` does not re-export are reached via `Prelude.X`. Do NOT
delete that line when editing a module.

## What `NriPrelude` re-exports (so you don't qualify it)

| Available bare from `NriPrelude` | Notes |
|---|---|
| `Bool`, `Maybe (..)`, `Result (..)`, `Char`, `Text`, `List` | types |
| `True`, `False`, `Just`, `Nothing`, `Ok`, `Err` | constructors |
| `Show`, `Eq`, `Ord`, `Num`, `Generic` | classes |
| `LT`, `GT`, `EQ`, `Ordering` | comparison |
| `Int`, `Float`, `Order` | numeric types - see `Int` warning below |
| `+ - * / // ^`, `== /=`, `< > <= >=`, `&& ||`, `++` | operators |
| `<\|`, `\|>`, `<<`, `>>` | application + composition (Elm style) |
| `<*>`, `>>=`, `fmap` | applicative / monad / functor essentials |
| `not`, `negate`, `abs`, `compare`, `max`, `min` | base helpers |
| `truncate`, `round`, `floor`, `ceiling`, `toFloat` | numeric conversions |
| `identity`, `always`, `xor`, `modBy`, `remainderBy`, `clamp` | Elm-flavour |
| `pi`, `sqrt`, `logBase`, trig functions | math |

## What you must reach via `Prelude.X` (NriPrelude does NOT re-export)

| Qualified | Why |
|---|---|
| `Prelude.IO`, `Prelude.FilePath`, `Prelude.String` | not in NriPrelude |
| `Prelude.Either`, `Prelude.Left`, `Prelude.Right` | NriPrelude prefers `Result` |
| `Prelude.Functor`, `Prelude.Applicative`, `Prelude.Monad` | classes not re-exported |
| `Prelude.Bounded`, `Prelude.Enum` | classes not re-exported |
| `Prelude.Integer`, `Prelude.Double` | types not re-exported (see warning) |
| `Prelude.pure`, `Prelude.show`, `Prelude.read` | not re-exported |
| `Prelude.length`, `Prelude.null`, `Prelude.head`, `Prelude.last`, `Prelude.init`, `Prelude.reverse` | base list ops |
| `Prelude.take`, `Prelude.drop`, `Prelude.takeWhile`, `Prelude.dropWhile`, `Prelude.span` | base list ops |
| `Prelude.replicate`, `Prelude.concat`, `Prelude.concatMap`, `Prelude.filter` | base list ops |
| `Prelude.foldr`, `Prelude.foldl`, `Prelude.sum`, `Prelude.all`, `Prelude.any`, `Prelude.elem` | base list ops |
| `Prelude.zip`, `Prelude.zipWith`, `Prelude.zipWith3` | base list ops |
| `Prelude.maybe`, `Prelude.const`, `Prelude.id` | base helpers |
| `Prelude.fromIntegral`, `Prelude.fromEnum`, `Prelude.toEnum`, `Prelude.maxBound`, `Prelude.minBound` | numerics |
| `Prelude.mod`, `Prelude.div` | base division |
| `Prelude.fst`, `Prelude.snd` | tuple accessors (`Tuple.first` / `Tuple.second` also work) |
| `Prelude.fmap`, `Prelude.mapM_`, `Prelude.traverse` | when you specifically want base behaviour |
| `Prelude.otherwise` | NriPrelude imports it but does not re-export |
| `Prelude..` (function composition with `.`) | NriPrelude uses `<<` / `>>` instead |
| `Prelude.<>` | monoidal append - NriPrelude does not re-export |
| `Prelude.>>=`, `Prelude.>>` | when you want base monad sequencing (rare; NriPrelude exports `>>=` directly, `>>` from base is hidden by NriPrelude's composition `>>`) |
| `Prelude.&&`, `Prelude.\|\|`, `Prelude.==`, `Prelude.<` etc | only when an instance must use base's class definition (e.g. in a `deriving` clause, NriPrelude's class IS base's class - you do not need qualifying for that case) |

## Critical gotchas

### `Int` vs `Int64`

`NriPrelude.Int` is **not** base's `Int`. NriPrelude defines:

```haskell
type Int = Data.Int.Int64
```

Base's `Int` (the platform-native machine word, 32 or 64 bits) is
reached as `Prelude.Int`. **The two are different types at the GHC
level**, even though they're numerically the same on a 64-bit machine.

This means:

- `posLine :: Int` from `Quone.Position` is `Int64`.
- `length :: [a] -> Prelude.Int` from base is base's native `Int`.
- A type signature using bare `Int` gets `Int64`. A signature using
  `Prelude.Int` gets the platform-native `Int`. **They will not
  unify.**
- Megaparsec, `Data.Map.size`, `Data.Text.length`, and most other
  base/Hackage libraries use base's `Int`. Anywhere you cross a
  boundary between NriPrelude code and one of those libraries you
  may need `Prelude.fromIntegral` to bridge.
- If GHC complains "couldn't match type `Int64` with `Int`" you have
  this problem. The fix is either to qualify the type as
  `Prelude.Int` everywhere on the relevant value's path, or to
  insert `Prelude.fromIntegral` at the call site that crosses the
  boundary.

### `<>` collisions

Several modules locally define a `<>` operator (e.g.
`Quone.Format.Doc`'s `Doc` concatenation, the formatter's
`<+>`). Those modules use:

```haskell
import NriPrelude hiding ((<>), (<+>))
import qualified Prelude
```

The `hiding` clause is required so the local `<>` wins. When editing
those files, `<>` always means the local definition; reach for
`Prelude.<>` if you need monoidal append on `Text`.

### `>>` is not Monad sequence

`NriPrelude` re-exports `>>` from `Internal.Shortcut`'s
`Basics`, where it is **left-to-right function composition** (the
mirror of `<<`). It is NOT the Monad sequence operator.

For monadic sequencing, use `do`-notation or `>>=`. For function
composition, prefer `>>` and `<<` over `Prelude..` (which is hidden
by the convention).

### `otherwise` is not in scope by default

Despite `Basics` importing it, `otherwise` is not re-exported by
`NriPrelude`. Use `Prelude.otherwise` in pattern guards:

```haskell
foo x
    | x > 0 = "positive"
    | Prelude.otherwise = "non-positive"
```

(Or reach for `Basics`'s `Order` predicates and avoid guards.)

### `True` / `False` work bare

These ARE re-exported via NriPrelude's `Bool (..)` re-export. You do
not need `Prelude.True` / `Prelude.False`.

### Show in deriving clauses uses `Prelude.Show` (the same class)

```haskell
data Foo = Foo
    deriving (Prelude.Show, Prelude.Eq, Prelude.Ord)
```

`Prelude.Show` and `NriPrelude`'s re-exported `Show` are the **same
class** (both come from `Prelude`). Either spelling derives the same
instance. The codebase convention is to use `Prelude.Show` etc. in
`deriving` clauses for clarity (it reads as "the standard Show").

## Why the project does not use a `Quone.Prelude` shim

A shim that re-exports `NriPrelude` plus the missing base names was
prototyped (in commit history under "tests-as-spec extractor work"
and again in "Prelude.X cleanup attempt") and pulled both times.
The blocker is the `Int` vs `Int64` semantic conflict above: a shim
that re-exposes both NriPrelude's `Int` and base's `Int` is
impossible without choosing one over the other, and either choice
breaks part of the codebase.

If you find yourself wanting bare `pure` / `show` / `True` / `<>` /
etc. without the `Prelude.` prefix, the right move is to scope your
desire to one module and add `import qualified Prelude as P` then
use `P.pure` etc. - shorter prefix, same idea, no library-wide
commitment.
